-- title:
--   Every central braid is a power of the full twist
-- statement:
--   Half of the computation of the centre of the braid group: for $n \ge 3$, every central element is a power of the full twist,
--
--   $$Z(B_n) \;\le\; \langle \Delta^2 \rangle, \qquad \Delta^2 = (\sigma_1 \sigma_2 \cdots \sigma_{n-1})^n.$$
--
--   The opposite inclusion is already available and needs no work: `TarchaBraids.prop_3_16_full_twist_mem_center` is proved and states that $\Delta^2$ is central, and the subgroup generated by a central element is contained in the centre. So all the content of Corollario 1.8.4 is in the direction stated here.
--
--   That the centre is no larger than $\langle \Delta^2 \rangle$ is a genuine theorem and is false for small $n$ in the sense that the hypothesis $n \ge 3$ is needed: $B_1$ and $B_2$ are abelian, so their centres are everything, while $\Delta^2$ generates only a proper subgroup of $B_2 \cong \mathbb{Z}$ --- indeed $\Delta^2 = \sigma_1^2$ there.
--
--   Two classical routes. The first is via the Artin representation: a central braid acts on the free group commuting with every $\mathrm{artinEndo}$, and one shows directly that such an automorphism must be the one induced by a power of the full twist, namely global conjugation by $x_1 x_2 \cdots x_n$. This route needs faithfulness of the representation to conclude, and so depends on `BraidsLinksMCG.artin_representation_injective`. The second is Garside-theoretic: in the Garside normal form $\Delta^k A$, centrality forces the tail $A$ to be trivial and $k$ to be even, using that conjugation by $\Delta$ acts on the generators by the flip $\sigma_i \mapsto \sigma_{n-i}$, which is an involution but not the identity for $n \ge 3$. That route is self-contained but requires the Garside structure to be set up first.
--
--   Geometrically the statement says the mapping class group of the punctured disc has centre generated by the Dehn twist about the boundary, which is why the braid group of the disc has infinite cyclic centre while the braid group of the sphere does not.
-- source:
--   Birman, Braids, Links and Mapping Class Groups, Chapter 1, Corollary 1.8.4; Chow, On the algebraical braid group, Ann. of Math. 49 (1948).

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BraidsLinksMCG_ArtinBraidGroup

namespace BraidsLinksMCG

theorem center_le_zpowers_full_twist (n : ℕ) (hn : 3 ≤ n) :
    Subgroup.center (ArtinBraidGroup n) ≤ Subgroup.zpowers (sigmaProd n ^ n) := by sorry

end BraidsLinksMCG
